1
00:00:00,450 --> 00:00:02,143
Lemon juice tastes sour.

2
00:00:02,143 --> 00:00:03,425
Milk tastes mild.

3
00:00:03,425 --> 00:00:05,838
An antacid tastes chalky and bitter.

4
00:00:06,239 --> 00:00:07,898
All three are mostly water.

5
00:00:07,898 --> 00:00:09,829
So what makes them so different?

6
00:00:10,279 --> 00:00:13,349
Diya squeezes a lemon in
her kitchen and wonders.

7
00:00:13,349 --> 00:00:15,681
Is the water itself hiding something?

8
00:00:16,031 --> 00:00:16,931
It is.

9
00:00:16,687 --> 00:00:19,106
Even pure water is never just water.

10
00:00:19,606 --> 00:00:23,730
By the end, you will read one small
number, the pH, and know how sour or

11
00:00:23,730 --> 00:00:25,158
how bitter a liquid is.

12
00:00:25,808 --> 00:00:27,932
Think of two friends passing a ball.

13
00:00:28,282 --> 00:00:33,548
One water molecule hands a tiny piece,
a proton, H plus, to its neighbour.

14
00:00:33,948 --> 00:00:37,345
The giver is left as a
hydroxide ion, OH minus.

15
00:00:37,345 --> 00:00:40,782
The taker becomes a hydronium
ion, H three O plus.

16
00:00:41,182 --> 00:00:43,424
And the ball can be passed back.

17
00:00:43,424 --> 00:00:46,465
So this is an equilibrium,
running both ways.

18
00:00:47,065 --> 00:00:49,450
Let's watch it on our molecule bench.

19
00:00:49,800 --> 00:00:54,194
Here is pure water at twenty five
degrees Celsius, two ninety eight kelvin.

20
00:00:54,594 --> 00:00:55,636
A proton jumps.

21
00:00:55,636 --> 00:00:57,898
One hydronium and one hydroxide appear.

22
00:00:57,898 --> 00:01:00,169
Then they meet again and become water.

23
00:01:00,569 --> 00:01:03,922
This happens all the time,
but to very few molecules.

24
00:01:03,922 --> 00:01:07,377
Only about two in every billion
are split at any moment.

25
00:01:07,776 --> 00:01:09,743
Every split makes one of each.

26
00:01:09,743 --> 00:01:12,558
So in pure water,
hydronium equals hydroxide.

27
00:01:12,558 --> 00:01:16,165
Both are one times ten to the
minus seven moles per litre.

28
00:01:16,765 --> 00:01:21,715
Multiply the two, and you get
the ionic product of water, K w.

29
00:01:22,065 --> 00:01:24,591
K w equals hydronium times hydroxide.

30
00:01:24,591 --> 00:01:28,975
One times ten to the minus fourteen,
at two ninety eight kelvin.

31
00:01:29,375 --> 00:01:31,539
K w changes with temperature.

32
00:01:31,539 --> 00:01:34,038
Warmer water splits a little more.

33
00:01:34,438 --> 00:01:37,904
If hydronium is more than
hydroxide, the solution is acidic.

34
00:01:37,904 --> 00:01:40,027
If they are equal, it is neutral.

35
00:01:40,027 --> 00:01:42,194
If hydronium is less, it is basic.

36
00:01:42,844 --> 00:01:46,548
But numbers like ten to the
minus seven are hard to read.

37
00:01:46,548 --> 00:01:47,905
So we use the pH.

38
00:01:47,905 --> 00:01:48,952
Step by step.

39
00:01:49,302 --> 00:01:50,202
Step one.

40
00:01:49,939 --> 00:01:52,534
pH is minus the log of the
hydronium concentration.

41
00:01:53,284 --> 00:01:54,184
Step two.

42
00:01:54,075 --> 00:01:58,356
For pure water, the log of ten to
the minus seven is minus seven.

43
00:01:59,106 --> 00:02:00,006
Step three.

44
00:01:59,910 --> 00:02:01,437
The minus sign flips it.

45
00:02:01,437 --> 00:02:03,514
So the pH of pure water is seven.

46
00:02:04,264 --> 00:02:05,164
Step four.

47
00:02:05,112 --> 00:02:08,578
In the same way, pOH is
minus the log of hydroxide.

48
00:02:09,328 --> 00:02:10,282
Step five.

49
00:02:10,282 --> 00:02:12,155
Take minus log of K w.

50
00:02:12,155 --> 00:02:16,265
pH plus pOH equals fourteen,
at two ninety eight kelvin.

51
00:02:17,015 --> 00:02:20,180
Now the pH scale, with real
values from your textbook.

52
00:02:20,530 --> 00:02:22,877
Lemon juice, about two point two.

53
00:02:22,877 --> 00:02:25,324
Tomato juice, about four point two.

54
00:02:25,324 --> 00:02:26,797
Black coffee, five.

55
00:02:27,197 --> 00:02:28,805
Milk, six point eight.

56
00:02:28,805 --> 00:02:30,166
Pure water, seven.

57
00:02:30,166 --> 00:02:32,212
Human blood, seven point four.

58
00:02:32,612 --> 00:02:34,779
Egg white, seven point eight.

59
00:02:34,779 --> 00:02:37,741
Milk of magnesia, an antacid, about ten.

60
00:02:38,141 --> 00:02:39,600
Below seven is acidic.

61
00:02:39,600 --> 00:02:41,012
Above seven is basic.

62
00:02:41,012 --> 00:02:44,654
And each step of one means ten
times more or less hydronium.

63
00:02:45,254 --> 00:02:46,154
Compare.

64
00:02:45,873 --> 00:02:47,354
One drink has pH three.

65
00:02:47,354 --> 00:02:48,652
Another has pH five.

66
00:02:49,052 --> 00:02:49,997
Pause and think.

67
00:02:49,997 --> 00:02:52,804
Which one has more hydronium,
and how many times more?

68
00:02:56,004 --> 00:02:56,904
pH three.

69
00:02:56,674 --> 00:02:58,650
Two steps down means ten times ten.

70
00:02:58,650 --> 00:03:00,447
One hundred times more hydronium.

71
00:03:01,097 --> 00:03:02,039
Remember this.

72
00:03:02,039 --> 00:03:03,438
Lower pH, more acid.

73
00:03:03,438 --> 00:03:04,978
pH plus pOH is fourteen.

74
00:03:05,678 --> 00:03:08,448
Now, one worked example from the textbook.

75
00:03:08,748 --> 00:03:12,730
A cola drink has hydronium concentration
three point eight times ten to

76
00:03:12,730 --> 00:03:14,767
the minus three moles per litre.

77
00:03:14,767 --> 00:03:15,717
Find its pH.

78
00:03:16,217 --> 00:03:20,707
pH equals minus log of three point
eight times ten to the minus three.

79
00:03:21,457 --> 00:03:24,514
Log of three point eight
is zero point five eight.

80
00:03:24,514 --> 00:03:27,015
So the log is minus two point four two.

81
00:03:27,765 --> 00:03:29,419
The minus sign flips it.

82
00:03:29,419 --> 00:03:31,177
pH is two point four two.

83
00:03:31,177 --> 00:03:32,141
Quite acidic.

84
00:03:32,891 --> 00:03:35,915
And pOH is fourteen
minus two point four two.

85
00:03:35,915 --> 00:03:37,596
Eleven point five eight.

86
00:03:38,246 --> 00:03:39,707
Now, the exam trap.

87
00:03:40,057 --> 00:03:44,383
Students say ten to the minus eight
molar hydrochloric acid has pH eight.

88
00:03:44,383 --> 00:03:46,434
But an acid can never be basic.

89
00:03:46,834 --> 00:03:49,451
Water already gives
ten to the minus seven.

90
00:03:49,451 --> 00:03:50,351
Add both.

91
00:03:50,197 --> 00:03:52,914
The pH comes out about
six point nine eight.

92
00:03:52,914 --> 00:03:54,077
Just below seven.

93
00:03:54,727 --> 00:03:56,056
Where is this asked?

94
00:03:56,056 --> 00:03:59,498
Boards ask you to find pH from
hydronium, or the reverse.

95
00:03:59,498 --> 00:04:03,173
Entrance tests mix pH, pOH and
K w, like the next question.

96
00:04:03,773 --> 00:04:04,892
Exam level now.

97
00:04:04,892 --> 00:04:08,798
At two ninety eight kelvin, a
solution has pOH ten point six.

98
00:04:08,798 --> 00:04:11,027
What is its hydronium concentration?

99
00:04:11,027 --> 00:04:12,193
Pause and solve.

100
00:04:17,593 --> 00:04:18,879
The answer is B.

101
00:04:18,879 --> 00:04:22,168
Four times ten to the
minus four moles per litre.

102
00:04:22,668 --> 00:04:23,659
First find pH.

103
00:04:23,659 --> 00:04:26,501
Fourteen minus ten point
six is three point four.

104
00:04:27,001 --> 00:04:29,356
Hydronium is ten to the
minus three point four.

105
00:04:29,856 --> 00:04:32,769
That is about four times
ten to the minus four.

106
00:04:32,769 --> 00:04:34,709
A used pOH as if it were pH.

107
00:04:35,309 --> 00:04:36,849
Here is your recap.

108
00:04:36,849 --> 00:04:38,431
Screenshot this card.

109
00:04:41,631 --> 00:04:43,817
Can you answer these three
questions without help?

110
00:04:44,217 --> 00:04:45,117
One.

111
00:04:44,605 --> 00:04:46,686
Why does pure water have a pH of seven?

112
00:04:47,486 --> 00:04:48,386
Two.

113
00:04:47,849 --> 00:04:51,005
pH four or pH six, which has more
hydronium, and how many times?

114
00:04:51,805 --> 00:04:52,705
Three.

115
00:04:52,337 --> 00:04:54,636
If the pH is nine, what is the pOH?

116
00:04:55,536 --> 00:04:59,089
Next time: vinegar and
hydrochloric acid are both acids.

117
00:04:59,089 --> 00:05:01,236
So why is one safe on a salad?

118
00:05:01,236 --> 00:05:04,461
We find out in Ionization
of Weak Acids and Bases.
